ARM: dts: xm72050200: describe the watchdog as the SP805 it actually is

The block at 0x12030000 is a stock ARM PrimeCell SP805. Read back from a
GK7201V200 (xm72010200) camera:

    0x12030fe0..0xfec  05 18 14 00   PeriphID = 0x00141805
    0x12030ff0..0xffc  0d f0 05 b1   PCellID  = 0xb105f00d

which is exactly the id/mask that drivers/watchdog/sp805_wdt.c matches on
(.id = 0x00141805, .mask = 0x00ffffff). The in-tree driver therefore binds
to this hardware as-is and gives a working /dev/watchdog.

The node was previously described with the vendor-private "xmedia,wdg"
binding, which no in-tree driver knows. It existed only for the out-of-tree
xm_wdt.ko, and that module cannot work on this kernel anyway: it hardcodes
Linux irq 37 at all four of its irq call sites (osal_request_irq, enable_irq,
disable_irq, osal_free_irq) instead of taking the interrupt from this node,
and irq 37 is not the watchdog here. It reads the device tree only to ioremap
the registers.

Nothing else loses its watchdog by this change. The other consumer of this
block on the family, openhisilicon's open_wdt, does not use the device tree
at all - it has a hardcoded "#define WDT_BASE 0x12030000" and osal_ioremap(),
with no compatible string and no interrupt.

The clocks the AMBA bus and the driver need are already present in this file
and are wired the same way for the SP804 dual timers on the same SoC:
clk_3m feeds the PrimeCell counters and clk_apb is the APB bus clock.

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/xm72050200.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/xm72050200.dtsi
@@ -512,11 +512,11 @@
 		};
 
 		wdg: wdg@0x12030000 {
-			compatible = "xmedia,wdg";
+			compatible = "arm,sp805", "arm,primecell";
 			reg = <0x12030000 0x1000>;
-			reg-names = "wdg";
 			interrupts = <0 2 4>;
-			interrupt-names = "wdg";
+			clocks = <&clk_3m>, <&clk_apb>;
+			clock-names = "wdog_clk", "apb_pclk";
 		};
 	};
 
